<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Info.plist for the macOS .app, wired up by `info_plist_path` in Dioxus.toml.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` is not optional dressing. macOS denies the
  microphone to a *bundled* app that declares no reason for wanting it, and it
  denies it silently: no prompt, no entry under System Settings > Privacy &
  Security > Microphone, and cpal hands back a device that produces nothing.
  That reads as "voice is broken", not as "permission was refused". It does not
  reproduce under `dx serve`, because an unbundled binary is attributed to the
  terminal that launched it and inherits that terminal's own grant — so voice
  works all through development and dies in the DMG.

  WHY THE WHOLE PLIST AND NOT JUST THAT ONE KEY
  Three config surfaces look like they do this, and two of them don't. Checked
  against dx 0.7.9 by building each way and dumping the result:

    * `[permissions] microphone = { … }`  — the unified permissions table. dx
      maps it into the plist on the `dx build` ios/macos platform path, but
      `dx bundle` for macos/dmg package types runs another bundler that never
      reads it. No effect.
    * `[application] macos_info_plist`    — documented as "dx will merge any
      required settings into this file". Same story: not consulted by the
      bundler. No effect.
    * `[bundle.macos] info_plist_path`    — works, but REPLACES the generated
      plist rather than merging into it. Point it at a file holding only the one
      key and the .app ships with no CFBundleExecutable and no icon.

  So every key dx would have generated has to be repeated here. The cost is
  drift: CFBundleShortVersionString / CFBundleVersion are duplicated from the
  crate version and do not follow it automatically. Update them together, or
  re-check this file if dx changes what it generates.

  LSMinimumSystemVersion stays at dx's own default (10.13) rather than being
  raised to 13.0 for ScreenCaptureKit — `sysaudio::scope()` already reports at
  runtime when system-audio capture is unavailable, which is the friendlier
  failure than refusing to launch.

  WHY App Transport Security IS DISABLED FOR WEB CONTENT
  The same dev/bundled asymmetry as the microphone, on the network instead of a
  device. ATS is read from the *bundle's* Info.plist, and its default policy
  refuses cleartext. Nothing this app speaks is TLS: the gateway, LiveKit and
  the rendezvous are all `ws://`, to endpoints the user picks at runtime. Native
  Rust sockets never consult ATS, so they are unaffected either way — WKWebView
  loads do, which splits the two halves of screen sharing apart in a bundled
  build only.

  What that looks like when it bites, because it does not look like a network
  policy: the webview cannot reach the SFU, so `dxScreen.connect` fails with
  "could not establish signal connection: Load failed" the moment you join a
  voice channel (which is when the screen token arrives, so the message looks
  unrelated to sharing), every share renders black including your own preview,
  and voice itself is fine because it never goes through WebKit. Under
  `dx serve` there is no bundle to read a policy from, so none is enforced and
  all of it works.

  `NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent` and not `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads`: every
  cleartext load in question originates in the webview, and this key is scoped
  to exactly those, leaving the default policy in force for anything the app
  itself might fetch later. Apple's precedence rules also make the blanket key
  ignored for web content whenever this one is present, so adding it would buy
  nothing. A per-domain exception cannot work here at all — the hosts are a
  self-host address, a rendezvous, or a friend's LAN IP, none of them known
  until runtime.

  NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription is prophylactic, not part of that fix: macOS 15
  and later gate LAN access behind a per-app grant, and a bundled app needs its
  own where an unbundled one inherits the terminal's. It is not what blocks a
  public-IP SFU, but it is the same class of bug waiting for `allow_lan` self-
  hosting and for friends joining across a LAN.

  WHY THE CAMERA NEEDS A REASON HERE *AND* AN ENTITLEMENT
  `NSCameraUsageDescription` is the microphone story again, one device over: a
  bundled app that asks for the camera without declaring why is denied silently,
  and `dx serve` will not reproduce it because an unbundled binary inherits the
  terminal's grant. Camera capture runs in the webview (`getUserMedia`, driven by
  the LiveKit JS SDK), and WKWebView reads this key from the *bundle*, so a build
  without it gets a permission error inside the webview rather than anything that
  names a plist.

  This key alone is NOT enough. Signed builds run under the hardened runtime, and
  that denies device access unless the app also carries
  `com.apple.security.device.camera`; see the long note in Entitlements.plist,
  which is where the microphone's matching entry earns its place. Two files have
  to agree for the camera to work in a signed bundle, and each fails in a way
  that looks like the other.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>English</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Discordia</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>Discordia</string>
	<key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
	<string>Discordia.icns</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>com.discordia.app</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>Discordia</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>0.1.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>0.1.0</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>10.13</string>
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Discordia uses your camera when you turn it on in a voice channel.</string>
	<key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Discordia connects to servers on your local network when you self-host or join one a friend is hosting.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Discordia uses your microphone for voice chat.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
